[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mClassification: architectural.[0m "Reusable across multiple forms" names structure this repo doesn't have — there's
  exactly one form ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m login) with a [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m hardcoded to [38;5;153musername[39m/[38;5;153mpassword[39m, and no shared validation module or
  second form to reuse it from. So this is the full path: questions → approaches → design → spec → plan. I'll keep the
  ceremony proportional to a ~30-line codebase.

  What I found:
  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — one login form, [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m, no bundler, no module system in the browser layer
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — browser globals; [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m checks two named fields, returns [38;5;153m{valid, error}[39m (first error only)
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — CommonJS, Node-side, unrelated to the form
  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — no deps, no test script, no tooling

  First question, on scope. The fork: whether this work delivers only the shared validation module (login rewired to it,
  second form hypothetical), or also a concrete second form that proves the reuse.

  - [1mExtract only[0m — smallest change, login keeps working, module exists. Cheap, but "reusable" stays unproven: the first
    real second form often reveals the interface is wrong.
  - [1mExtract + a second form[0m — the second consumer is what actually validates the design. More work, and I'd need to know
    which form (signup? profile? search?) so I'm not inventing requirements.
  - [1mExtract + a throwaway second form in tests[0m — proves reuse against a second field set without shipping UI you didn't
    ask for.

  I'd recommend the third: it gets the design pressure of a second consumer without me guessing at a feature you didn't
  request.
